   "content": "The lighthouse, one of humanity\u2019s oldest aids to navigation, has a history stretching back thousands of years. The most famous ancient example is the Pharos of Alexandria, built around 280 BCE in Egypt. Standing perhaps over 100 meters tall, it used a fire at its summit to guide ships into the busy harbor and became one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.\n\nFor centuries, lighthouses relied on open fires, then candles and oil lamps, often placed atop stone towers on dangerous coastlines. Their keepers lived isolated lives, maintaining the light through storms and darkness. In the 18th and 19th centuries, construction improved dramatically with masonry towers and Fresnel lenses, which focused light into powerful beams visible for many miles.\n\nThe 20th century brought automation. Electric lights, acetylene gas, and later solar power reduced the need for resident keepers, and many lighthouses were automated or decommissioned as GPS and electronic navigation transformed seafaring. Today, while few remain staffed, lighthouses endure as symbols of safety, solitude, and maritime heritage, preserved as museums and cultural landmarks around the world.",
