   "content": "The cobblestones are slick with morning damp, worn smooth by centuries of boots and cartwheels. Gulls wheel overhead, their cries echoing off the close-packed houses that lean together like old conspirators. Salt air mingles with the smell of fresh bread from a corner bakery, and somewhere a rope halyard clinks against a mast.\n\nDown at the quay, fishing boats nudge against tyre-covered pilings, their paint faded to the colours of weathered shells. An old man mends nets with hands like knotted rope, nodding as I pass. Beyond him, the harbour opens to a steel-grey sea, dotted with the white triangles of distant sails.\n\nI turn up a narrow lane where flower boxes spill geraniums over stone walls, and a cat watches from a windowsill with ancient indifference. The town feels suspended between tides\u2014part land, part water, wholly itself. At the top of the hill, a squat church tower keeps its long watch over the rooftops, and I pause to look back at the maze of streets below, listening to the sea breathe against the harbour wall.",
