   "content": "The alarm sounds at 4:30 a.m., long before the sun crests the ridge. Elias pulls on his suit, the fabric stiff with old wax and propolis. The bees are already awake, humming in the dark.\n\nHis days follow the bloom. In spring, he chases the wild plum and blackberry, moving hives by truck under cover of night when the foragers are home. Summer means the heavy clover flow, and Elias spends hours in the bee yard, lifting frames heavy with honey, watching for queen cells, and squinting at drones tumbling from the entrance. He knows each colony by temperament\u2014the gentle Italians, the defensive Carniolans.\n\nBy August, the goldenrod comes, and the honey house smells of sweet, hot air. He uncaps frames with a warm knife, spinning honey from the combs until his arms ache.\n\nWinter is quiet but not idle. He repairs boxes, melts wax, and reads the bees through the glass of the observation hive. He loses some colonies each year, and it never gets easier. But on the first warm day of March, when the maples bloom and the bees return with pollen on their legs, Elias remembers why he stays.",
