   "content": "Chess is played on an 8x8 board between two players, White and Black. Each begins with 16 pieces: one king, one queen, two rooks, two bishops, two knights, and eight pawns. White moves first, and players alternate turns.\n\nEach piece moves differently. The king moves one square in any direction. The queen moves any distance along ranks, files, or diagonals. Rooks move along ranks and files; bishops along diagonals. Knights move in an L-shape\u2014two squares one way and one square perpendicular\u2014and can jump over pieces. Pawns move forward one square (or two from their starting square), capture diagonally, and may be promoted to any piece except a king upon reaching the far rank.\n\nThe goal is checkmate: placing the opponent's king under attack with no legal escape. A king may not move into check. Special moves include castling (king and rook together, under conditions) and en passant (a special pawn capture). Stalemate\u2014no legal move while not in check\u2014results in a draw, as do repetition, the fifty-move rule, and insufficient material.",
