   "content": "A river forms a delta when it reaches a standing body of water\u2014a sea, lake, or ocean\u2014and its flow slows dramatically. Losing velocity, the river can no longer carry its full load of sediment, so it deposits material in layers. Coarser sand and gravel settle first near the mouth, while finer silt and clay travel farther before dropping out. Over time, these deposits build up and choke the channel, forcing the river to split into distributaries that fan outward. Each branch deposits its own sediment, extending the delta farther into the water. Accumulation continues until the delta rises above the surface, forming low, flat land. The shape depends on the balance between river sediment supply, wave action, and tides: strong waves produce smooth, arcuate deltas, while weak wave action allows birdsfoot deltas with long distributaries to form. Vegetation eventually colonizes the new land, stabilizing sediment and helping the delta grow.",
